{"id":"u-0af16c9a-4fab-43ae-a9cf-4647fc5bd8ee","src":"community","title":"5x5 Progressive Overload: Build Strength That Last","author":"Dylan L.","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/82Of8t-untitled-program","desc":"5x5 Progressive Overload: Build Strength That Lasts\n\nMaster the movement. Build the foundation. Earn the weight.\n\nThis program builds strength, muscle, and long-term resilience through gradual progressive overload. Instead of chasing heavy weights from day one, you'll develop technique, confidence, and consistency before the loads become challenging.\n\nMAIN LIFTS: 5x5\n\nThe primary lifts are barbell rows, belt squats, dumbbell or barbell bench press, and rack pulls.\n\nStart at approximately 50% of your estimated 5x5 working weight. It should feel easy! Five sets at a manageable load give you plenty of practice to refine your technique and master each movement.\n\nGradually add weight as you progress, provided you can complete all five sets with solid form. The goal isn't to test your strength every session; it's to build it over time.\n\nACCESSORIES: 3-WEEK PROGRESSION\n\nStart light and keep the same weight for three weeks:\n\n- Week 1: 3 sets of 8\n- Week 2: 3 sets of 10\n- Week 3: 3 sets of 12\n\nAfter completing 3x12 with good technique, increase the weight by approximately 5 pounds and return to 3x8.\n\nThis slower progression builds muscle while gradually increasing the loading demands on tendons and other connective tissues.\n\n10-SECOND ISOMETRIC HOLDS\n\nAt the END of each working set, immediately after your final repetition, hold the weight for 10 seconds in the prescribed top position, maintaining muscular tension rather than resting on a full joint lockout where appropriate.\n\nExample: Perform 5 reps, then hold for 10 seconds before resting. The hold comes AFTER the set's repetitions—not between reps.\n\nTendon researcher Dr. Keith Baar has highlighted the role of sustained mechanical tension in tendon adaptation. Although a 10-second hold has not been proven to recruit every tendon fiber, it offers a practical way to add sustained loading that may support tendon resilience, stability, and confidence under load.\n\nChoose a safe hold position for each exercise and never push through pain.\n\nTHE PHILOSOPHY\n\nMuscles and connective tissues adapt on different timelines. By combining 5x5 strength work, gradual accessory progression, and controlled isometric holds, this program prioritizes sustainable progress over short-term ego lifting.\n\nStart light. Master the movement. Trust the progression. Build strength that lasts.","level":["Intermediate","Advanced"],"goals":["Strength","Muscle"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":90,"joins":2,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-31T16:33:03.192Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Day 1","Day 2","Day 3","Day 4","Day 5"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Barbell Row","sets":[{"r":5,"x":5}]},{"n":"Kelso Shrug (Chest Supported)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Pull-Up (Assisted)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Bicep Curl (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Preacher Curl (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Straight Arm Pulldown","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Seated Calf Raise","sets":[{"r":10,"x":3}]},{"n":"Belt Squat","sets":[{"r":5,"x":5}]},{"n":"Belt Squat","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Hip Adductor (Machine)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Leg Extension","sets":[{"r":10,"x":3}]},{"n":"Ab Wheel","sets":[{"r":10,"x":3}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Bench Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":5,"x":5}]},{"n":"Dip (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Incline Bench Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Overhead Tricep Extension (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Tricep Pushdown (Cable)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Back Extension (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":10,"x":3}]},{"n":"Rack Pull (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":5,"x":5}]},{"n":"Single Leg Romanian Deadlift","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Lying Leg Curl","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Hang